osx-gpu: radeonsi on a big-endian host, GFX8: index buffers byte-swapped for the fetch

An index buffer holds host-order indices on a big-endian host (part one).
GFX6-7 swap them as they fetch (VGT_DMA_INDEX_TYPE.SWAP_MODE, part three);
GFX8 fetches index buffers through L2 and ignores that field. Measured on
Polaris 12 (Firebrand's E9173, 2026-11-13) with rdn_gltest -L: 16- and
32-bit indices from client memory and from buffer objects drew nothing for
every SWAP_MODE 0-3, set through PKT3_INDEX_TYPE and again as the context
register, with REQ_PATH and RDREQ_POLICY varied as well; the same indices
stored little-endian drew right with any of them; 8-bit indices (native on
GFX8, nothing to swap) drew right. Display lists, which st/mesa replays as
indexed draws, and everything Mesa turns into an indexed draw drew nothing.

So on GFX8 the draw reads a byte-swapped copy of the indices it uses, made
the way GFX7's ubyte indices are already widened (si_draw_vbo): user
indices are swapped on the CPU as they are uploaded, and a buffer object's
by a compute shader (si_create_index_swap_compute_shader, internal, so the
big-endian memory lowering leaves its loads alone) into a new buffer that
keeps the indices at their offsets. Indirect draws take the range
si_get_draw_start_count reads, as the ubyte path does.

Gated on GFX_VERSION == GFX8 inside the per-generation draw code: the GFX6
and GFX7 instantiations compile as before. The rdn winsys serves GFX8 only
where the kext sets RDN_VRAM_INFO64_LE_IBS (Firebrand), so this path is
behind that flag as well.

The swapped copy of a buffer object is kept with the buffer (si_resource
be_index_swap), made once by one dispatch over the whole buffer and made
again only after the buffer is written: a CPU write map or buffer_subdata,
new storage, or a GPU write through si_barrier_after_internal_op (copies,
clears). Buffers radeonsi cannot watch -- persistently mapped (glthread's
upload buffers among them), bound as an SSBO, image or streamout target,
over 16 MB, or rewritten more than 8 times -- are swapped per draw, just
the range the draw reads, into the stream uploader. The first version made
a new buffer per draw sized to the range's end: on glthread's upload
buffer that ran the card out of video memory (frb_glbench draws-c on the
E9173, 2026-11-13). The copy is used only by the context whose command
stream made it.

